WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:02.401
(engaging music)

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:05.809
- The first Scorsese film I
watched was "Taxi Driver".

00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:08.820
I watched it with my dad
and I think I was about 15.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.000
And we watched a couple of
films over that weekend,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.227
and I said, "Dad, I think I really wanna

00:00:12.227 --> 00:00:13.670
"have a go at this acting."

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:14.503
And he was, "Do you?"

00:00:14.503 --> 00:00:16.450
I went, "Yeah I really do, I'm honest."

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:17.283
And he went, "Okay."

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.020
And we went to the video
shop, and we got three films

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.110
and one of which was "Taxi Driver".

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:22.610
The others, one was "The Godfather"

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.110
and one was "Deer Hunter".

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.100
So straight away I was kind of immersed

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.520
in classic filmmaking,
do you know what I mean?

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.840
Beautiful storytelling, strong characters.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.870
And stories which meant
something, which had a voice

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.005
and which was talking
about the human condition.

00:00:38.005 --> 00:00:40.460
(slow jazz music)

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.700
For me, "Taxi Driver" just
literally blew my mind.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.090
I'd never seen that world,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.409
I hadn't seen New York in that way before,

00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:51.760
but to see that richness of that world

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.333
and the darkness and the depth

00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:56.420
of what he captured just
absolutely blew my mind

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.720
and a performance which
it's completely outstanding.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.240
And to watch Travis Bickle go through

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.960
that massive journey he goes
on, do you know what I mean?

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:04.793
His hatred for the world

00:01:04.793 --> 00:01:09.730
and then his feeling a part
of the outside of society

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.860
and, that beautiful opening,

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.560
and that music at the beginning,
do you know what I mean?

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.470
The orchestral music would begin

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.023
and the lights of the city.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.173
I was mesmerized.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.244
You know where Harvey
Keitel stood on a stoop

00:01:28.244 --> 00:01:30.650
and he comes in, and De Niro comes in

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:31.506
and they have little chat.

00:01:31.506 --> 00:01:33.443
- Is your name Matthew?

00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:34.513
Need some action?

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.150
- Officer, I swear I'm clean,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.030
I'm just waiting here for a friend.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.368
- And just to see him in
that hat and that vest

00:01:44.368 --> 00:01:47.570
and the imagery of the
characters and stuff,

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.343
it really was amazing.

00:01:49.343 --> 00:01:52.120
(upbeat jazz music)

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.140
The next film I watched straight away

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.643
which was literally a week
later, I watched "Mean Streets"

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.757
and that was...

00:02:01.757 --> 00:02:02.670
(blows burst of air)

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.733
It was a world I knew nothing about,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.860
but was obsessed with then,
do you know what I mean?

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.240
And obsessed with that whole era

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.290
and that culture in America at that time.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.300
Yeah, a lot of British
actors were kinda brought up

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.708
on that stuff as well,
do you know what I mean?

00:02:17.708 --> 00:02:20.025
And it really resonated.

00:02:20.025 --> 00:02:22.370
But the character study was amazing.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.480
I'd never seen character studies like that

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.313
do you know what I mean?

00:02:25.313 --> 00:02:27.741
And then I started watching
a lot of films after that.

00:02:27.741 --> 00:02:31.120
But to see the way these people

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.338
were portrayed was breathtaking.

00:02:33.338 --> 00:02:35.367
(traditional Italian music)

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:38.990
"Goodfellas" is still I mean
it's in my top five films

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.514
of all time, do you know what I mean?

00:02:40.514 --> 00:02:43.370
And again, that, all the
different characters,

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.767
all the colors of the
tapestry that he puts together

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:47.564
of all these different people.

00:02:47.564 --> 00:02:49.130
And the thing is,

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.490
the thing about Marty's
films in particular,

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.740
is they're never one dimensional
these characters at all,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.110
do you know what I mean?

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:56.943
Been lucky enough to work with him,

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.380
that's kind of that's
the process what he does.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.810
He always tries to help you find a way

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.620
of making your characters
humane, do you know what I mean?

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.850
Even if they're psychotic killers

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.560
or whatever in any respect.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.873
He always tries to find
this other element,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.140
to show that the humanity
within that character

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.407
at some point or at some
level do you know what I mean?

00:03:18.407 --> 00:03:19.460
And these are people that are...

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.424
Look, these are people
that are shooting people.

00:03:21.424 --> 00:03:22.280
Do you know what I mean?

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.879
They're killing people,
they're robbing things,

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:27.434
they're stealing this,
they're killing people.

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:30.260
(gun shots)
But yet we go on

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.093
that journey with them,

00:03:31.093 --> 00:03:31.926
do you know what I mean?

00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:33.410
They become likable,
(car explodes)

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.533
although we shouldn't like them.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.240
As a fact, we shouldn't
like these kind of people

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.010
but we do, we get immersed in that story,

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:42.843
do you know what I mean?

00:03:42.843 --> 00:03:43.930
And that's what he does,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.620
he makes the characters gregarious,

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.610
you're really drawn to them characters,

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.030
and you end up rooting
especially, in "Goodfellas"

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.202
you end up rooting for them,
do you know what I mean?

00:03:53.202 --> 00:03:55.020
'cause he's a good man,

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.532
but it's the world he gets caught up in.

00:03:57.532 --> 00:04:00.070
(traditional Italian music)

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.557
Obviously, there was
the scene with Joe Pesci

00:04:03.557 --> 00:04:05.030
"Clown, do I amuse you?"

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.570
That I mean that, when I first
saw it, I was blown away.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:10.453
- I'm funny how, I mean
funny like I'm a clown,

00:04:10.453 --> 00:04:11.493
I amuse you.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.480
I make you laugh, I'm
here to fucking amuse you.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.940
What do you mean funny?

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.363
Funny how, how am I funny?

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:23.187
- I'm not, just you know
how you tell the story what?

00:04:23.187 --> 00:04:24.830
- I watched it, and I think I watched it

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.430
in the cinema first,

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.242
and then when I got it on video,

00:04:28.242 --> 00:04:31.110
I just remember rewinding
that a good few times I did

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.910
watch that scene, and just
how he (clicks fingers) flips.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:35.959
- You're a funny guy.

00:04:35.959 --> 00:04:37.131
(laughs)

00:04:37.131 --> 00:04:37.964
- What do you mean?

00:04:37.964 --> 00:04:39.233
You mean do I talk, what?

00:04:40.303 --> 00:04:42.770
- Just, you know, you're just funny.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.780
It's you're funny yeah,

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.800
the way you tell the
story, and everything.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.633
- Funny how?

00:04:47.633 --> 00:04:48.880
(traditional Italian music)
- He just goes.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.913
But then again, you feel uncomfortable

00:04:52.913 --> 00:04:54.830
for the other characters as well.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.121
But you're kind of excited
about what he's gonna do,

00:04:58.121 --> 00:04:59.690
do you know what I mean?

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:01.610
So that was one particular
scene I do watch it

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:02.980
over and over again.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.704
- Get the fuck out of here, Tommy.

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:05.607
- You motherfucker!

00:05:05.607 --> 00:05:07.742
I almost had 'em, I almost had 'em.

00:05:07.742 --> 00:05:10.363
(funky music)

00:05:10.363 --> 00:05:13.820
- It's his storytelling both visually

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:17.380
and with the characters that
he tells his stories with,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:18.213
do you know what I mean?

00:05:18.213 --> 00:05:19.430
You just become immersed in it.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.870
He is a master at telling stories,

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:23.703
do you know what I mean?

00:05:23.703 --> 00:05:27.620
Like I said about characters
with faults, with defects,

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:28.453
do you know what I mean?

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:32.720
He portrays the human
condition beautifully I think.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.170
There's very few for me.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.580
For me, he's the best director there is.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.610
To be able to work with him a few times

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:43.110
has been absolutely unbelievable.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.808
And when I work with him
on "Gangs of New York"

00:05:46.808 --> 00:05:49.167
on my last day he said to me,

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:50.950
"We'll do this again sometime."

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.050
And I was, "You can't say
that to me and not do it."

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.950
He was, "No, I promise you,
we'll do this again sometime."

00:05:57.187 --> 00:05:58.615
Yeah, and he stuck to his word.

00:05:58.615 --> 00:06:02.306
And I'm extremely humbled and very honored

00:06:02.306 --> 00:06:05.360
to be able to work with
such a wonderful man.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:06.250
- [Interviewer] Did he
say that to you at the end

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.550
of "The Irishman" as well?

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.970
- I can't say.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.280
No, yeah, yeah, oh, yeah.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:13.113
No, no.

00:06:13.113 --> 00:06:16.303
Hmm, hmm, yeah, yeah.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:18.925
Yeah.

00:06:18.925 --> 00:06:21.425
(funky music)

